WEBVTT

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:01.067
Hi, my name's Selina.

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:05.338
I'm a third year studying Electrical
and Computer Engineering at Carnegie Mellon University.

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:08.775
One of Carnegie Mellon University's
longest held traditions is Buggy.

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:13.546
Buggy is an annual student-led relay race.
The race is known as sweepstakes.

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:18.284
And essentially what happens is that
we put students inside a torpedo-like structure

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:22.722
and have them travel at
30 miles per hour along a flagstaff hill.

00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:24.290
Initially in first year,

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:30.263
my friend was scouted to become a driver
and drivers have to be under five feet pretty much

00:00:30.263 --> 00:00:34.634
because drivers have to be
put inside this really small structure

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:37.570
that travels along this
hill and to optimize it,

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.540
optimize speed and wind
resistance and whatnot,

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:44.577
the Buggy is really slim and drivers
have to lie down on their belly.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.413
My friend was scouted as a driver,

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:49.249
but as the electrical
engineering student that I am,

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.683
I wanted to be a mechanic.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:53.787
So there's actually three roles
that you can take on most Buggy teams,

00:00:53.787 --> 00:00:56.456
and it's either being
a driver, being mechanic or a pusher.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:58.425
Drivers are ones that are
actually in the Buggy.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:01.561
Mechanics are the ones that
are designing the Buggy,

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:05.365
optimizing the Buggy shape
to account for speed,

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:09.235
and also making sure that drivers
are safely hooked into the Buggy.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.236
There's also pushers.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.739
These are the people that are
actually pushing the Buggy uphill.

00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:15.041
And then once the Buggy gets
towards the downhill portion,

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.509
the drivers are able to steer.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.011
So it's kind of like a baton race.

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:19.779
There's five sections of the hill.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.047
And as a mechanic,

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:25.351
I was able to meet other students in my
major that I wouldn't normally have met

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:27.187
because they were just
so much older than me.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:32.292
So there were third years and fourth years
who were in Buggy for multiple years,

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:36.563
and I was able to get some advice from
them, career advice, academic advice,

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:41.034
and just learn more about college life
from people who have been here longer.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.403
Some teams take Buggy really seriously,

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:48.074
and so a lot of teams
require a 4 AM and 5 AM wake-up call

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:51.845
just because that's when
the road is officially closed for Buggy testing.

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:55.682
I was in Buggy for two semesters.
It was a really fun experience.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:02:00.353
I got to meet a lot of different people
from different majors and also from different years.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.521
So I highly recommend,

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:05.058
and I think it's a really unique
tradition that just belongs to CMU.